.TH GIT-CLEANUP-REPO 1 "2026-06-13" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-cleanup-repo \- report stale local branch cleanup candidates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup-repo
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git cleanup-repo
resolves the remote fetch endpoint, fetches the exact repository default branch
without updating remote-tracking refs or FETCH_HEAD, then reports stale local
branch candidates with exact object IDs.
.PP
By default it reports local branches that are already merged into the base
branch. This includes squash-, rebase-, and cherry-pick-merged branches: patch
IDs identify candidate matches, and the exact net tree delta must also be
present in a pinned base snapshot. A clean squash merge is recognized even
though its commits are not ancestors of the base, while byte-, mode-, or later
same-path differences are excluded. Branch and upstream-state enumeration must
complete before candidates are reported. No local branch or worktree is deleted
automatically because Git cannot portably serialize ref deletion with an
already-resolved checkout. It can also report branches whose existing upstream
tracking state is gone or every local branch except the base branch. The command
does not execute configured fetch or prune mappings; other remote-tracking state
must be refreshed separately when needed.
.PP
Base selection does not depend on configured fetch refspecs or short ref names.
The command fetches exactly
.BR refs/heads/ base
without tags, validates and pins its native SHA-1 or SHA-256 object ID, and
checks the local fast-forward relationship before switching branches. It then
creates or fast-forwards the base using that same object ID. A divergent base
fails before the current branch or local base is changed. Dry-run uses isolated
temporary object storage.
.PP
The command retains the single raw configured fetch URL for both remote
inspection and fetch, allowing Git to apply any
.B insteadOf
rewrite exactly once per operation. Remotes with zero or multiple fetch URLs
are rejected. Git 2.29 or newer is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fIbranch\fR
Base branch to keep and update.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remote " " \fIname\fR
Remote fetch endpoint to resolve and fetch from. Defaults to
.BR origin .
.TP
.B \-\-gone
Also report branches whose upstream is gone.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Report all local branches except the base branch.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-worktrees
Accepted for compatibility; no worktree or branch is removed.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH SAFETY
The command refuses to run with a dirty current worktree or active
rebase, merge, cherry-pick, or revert state. Automatic local branch and
linked-worktree removal is disabled even with
.BR \-\-remove\-worktrees ,
because Git does not provide the serialization needed to preserve an in-flight
checkout safely.
